---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import plates from "../data/plates.json";
const naa = [
  ["J25","1971/9830","Falco, Carmine; wife Francesca [nee Zampiello]; and child Giuseppina [known as Josephine]","1957–1977","5115541"],
  ["J25","1972/4964","Falco, Raffaele [Italian, born 1925]; wife Caterina [nee Zampiello]; and children Filomena and Antonio","1959–1979","5054689"],
  ["J25","1964/1364","Zampiello, Angelo","1964","1692778"],
  ["J25","1972/5134","Servodio, Angelo Michele; wife Raffaela [nee Zampiello]; and children Maria, Angelo","1960–1976","5072004"],
  ["A2478","MICELI U","MICELI Umberto b. 1934; Filomena (nee Zampiello) b. 24 Aug 1932 — per ship ROMA, 1960","1960","1415472"],
];
const graves = [
  ["Falco","Carmine","","27 Apr 1985","CHAP-082-B2","Carminantonio, b. Arienzo 1901"],
  ["Falco","Filomena","","10 Aug 1988","CHAP-082-B1","Filomena Annecchino, b. 1897 — beside her husband"],
  ["Falco","Raffaele","","interred 30 Sep 1988","V-0946","b. 1925, son of Carminantonio"],
  ["Falco","Caterina","7 Jul 1925","14 Feb 2004","V-0947","Caterina Zampiello, Raffaele's wife"],
  ["Falco","Francesca","27 Apr 1928","10 Sep 2022","V-0949","Francesca Zampiello, Carmine's wife"],
];
---
<Base title="Australia">
  <h1>Australia</h1>
  <p class="lede">The Italian half of this archive was built out of parish registers. The Australian half
  was built out of a cemetery index, and it answered a question the registers could not.</p>

  <h2>Two brothers married two sisters</h2>
  <p>This is the finding. The National Archives of Australia holds, in its Brisbane office, the
  immigration files of both couples:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <strong>Falco, Carmine; wife Francesca [nee Zampiello]; and child Giuseppina [known as Josephine]</strong><br/>
    <strong>Falco, Raffaele [Italian, born 1925]; wife Caterina [nee Zampiello]; and children Filomena and Antonio</strong>
  </blockquote>
  <p><strong>Carmine</strong> (born Forchia, 1930) and <strong>Raffaele</strong> (born 1925) were
  brothers — both sons of Carminantonio Falco and Filomena Annecchino. <strong>Francesca</strong> (born
  Arpaia, 1928) and <strong>Caterina</strong> (born 7 July 1925) were both Zampiello. Two Falco brothers
  of Forchia married two Zampiello sisters of Arpaia, and both couples came to Brisbane.</p>
  <p>And they were not the only sisters to come. <strong>Raffaela Zampiello</strong> arrived with her
  husband Angelo Michele Servodio; <strong>Filomena Zampiello</strong>, born 24 August 1932, arrived on
  the <em>Roma</em> in 1960 with her husband Umberto Miceli under the Australian–Italian Assisted
  Migration Agreement. Their father <strong>Angelo Zampiello</strong> has a Brisbane file of his own,
  dated 1964.</p>
  <p class="small">Giuseppina is recorded in her family's own immigration file as
  <em>"known as Josephine"</em> — the small administrative act by which an Italian child becomes an
  Australian one.</p>

  <h2>The voyage, 26 March 1964</h2>
  <p>The passenger index names <strong>four</strong> Falcos on one voyage:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Arrived</th><th>Port</th><th>Ship</th><th>Embarked</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Falco, Carmine</strong></td><td class="mono">26 Mar 1964</td><td>Brisbane</td><td><em>Sydney</em></td><td>Naples</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Falco, Francesca</strong></td><td class="mono">26 Mar 1964</td><td>Brisbane</td><td><em>Sydney</em></td><td>Naples</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Falco, Giuseppina</strong></td><td class="mono">26 Mar 1964</td><td>Brisbane</td><td><em>Sydney</em></td><td>Naples</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Falco, Antonio</strong></td><td class="mono">26 Mar 1964</td><td>Brisbane</td><td><em>Sydney</em></td><td>Naples</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Carmine, Francesca, and their children <strong>Giuseppina</strong> — eight years old — and
  <strong>Antonio</strong>. Antonio does not appear in the immigration file, which names only Giuseppina;
  he is almost certainly the Anthony Falco of the family record, and this is the first document to place
  him on the voyage.</p>
  <p>The <strong><em>Sydney</em></strong> was a <strong>Flotta Lauro</strong> liner, put on the
  Europe–Australia migrant run in 1951 together with her sister the <em>Roma</em>, carrying 800
  passengers from Genoa, Naples and Messina. A contemporary account has the pair setting
  <q>a whole new standard in migrant transportation</q>. It is a small coincidence worth noting that
  Francesca's sister <strong>Filomena Zampiello came out on the <em>Roma</em></strong> four years
  earlier — the two sisters crossed on sister ships.</p>
  <p class="small">The passenger list is digitised: <span class="mono">NAA: K269, 26 MAR 1964
  SYDNEY</span>, 42 pages, item 12100311. The images require a RecordSearch session to view.</p>

  <h2>The files</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Series</th><th>Control symbol</th><th>Title</th><th>Dates</th><th>Item ID</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{naa.map(([s,c,t,d,i]) => (
      <tr><td class="mono">{s}</td><td class="mono">{c}</td><td>{t}</td><td class="mono">{d}</td>
        <td class="mono">{i}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small"><strong>J25</strong> is the Brisbane series of naturalisation and immigration case
  files. None of these are digitised — they exist on paper in Brisbane and can be ordered or read in
  person. They are the single most promising unread source for this family.</p>

  <h2>Nudgee Cemetery</h2>
  <p>Five Falco burials, with exact dates and plot numbers — and two corrections to what the family
  record said.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th><th>Plot</th><th>Who</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{graves.map(([s,g,b,d,l,w]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{g} {s}</strong></td><td class="mono">{b}</td><td class="mono">{d}</td>
        <td class="mono">{l}</td><td>{w}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>Carminantonio Falco died on 27 April 1985, not in 1988.</strong> The family record carried
  1988 — which is in fact the year his wife died. And <strong>Filomena Annecchino's death date, blank in
  the family record, was 10 August 1988</strong>. They lie in adjacent niches, CHAP-082-B1 and B2.</p>
  <p>Raffaele, Caterina and Francesca lie in adjacent plots — V-0946, V-0947, V-0949.</p>

  <h2>How this was found</h2>
  <p>Among the photographs attached to the family tree was one that was not a photograph: a scan of a
  typed <strong>burial index dated 2 January 1991</strong>, listing four Falcos with page numbers. That
  scrap named a Caterina and a Raffaele Falco nobody had accounted for. Searching the Nudgee registers
  for those two names produced the plot numbers and dates; searching the National Archives for
  <em>Zampiello</em> — a surname with about 123 bearers in the world — produced the immigration files
  that explained who they were.</p>

  <h2>Plates</h2>
  <div class="grid two">
    {plates.map(p => (
      <figure style="margin:0" class="card">
        <img src={u("/plates/" + p.f)} alt={p.t} loading="lazy"
             style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:.35rem;display:block" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:.6rem">
          <strong>{p.t}</strong><br/><span class="small">{p.c}</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="small">From the family's own scans. <strong>Living people are omitted from this build</strong> —
  a further nineteen photographs in the family tree show living members and are not published here.</p>

  <h2>Still to do</h2>
  <p class="small">These, and every other open question in the archive, are collected at
  <a href="../open-questions/">Open questions</a>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Order the J25 files.</strong> Now that the ship and date are known, these files should
    add the sponsor, the addresses, the occupations, and often photographs. Both
    <span class="mono">J25, 1971/9830</span> and <span class="mono">J25, 1972/4964</span> can be
    requested; 1972/4964 is already marked <em>Open</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the 42 digitised pages</strong> of <span class="mono">NAA: K269, 26 MAR 1964
    SYDNEY</span> to find the family's own line — age, occupation, and intended address in Australia.</li>
    <li><strong>Angelo Zampiello's 1964 Brisbane file</strong> may settle whether he emigrated or was
    merely sponsored — and may carry his Abyssinia service.</li>
    <li><strong>Giovanni Zampiello</strong>, born 1 December 1911, appears in three files as an Italian
    prisoner of war or internee in Australia, 1939–46, PWI60047. Whether he is kin is untested — but
    Zampiello is a one-village name, and that village is Arpaia.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
